      "id": 1,
      "text": "National City was founded in 1868 when Frank Kimball and his brothers bought Rancho de la Nación, laying the groundwork for the city.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_City%2C_California"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1885 the Santa Fe Railroad made National City its terminus, and the National City Depot still stands as the surviving Italianate station of the five transcontinental termini.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        8,
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.visitcalifornia.com/places-to-visit/national-city/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The National City Public Library’s Kile Morgan Local History Room holds original archives, maps, and yearbooks that chronicle the city’s early families and development.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_City%2C_California"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "National City’s name traces back to the Mexican era when the land was called Rancho de la Nación, meaning “Ranch of the Nation.”.",
